## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of urbanization and industrialization, water pollution has become a serious environmental problem [1–3]. A variety of refractory organic pollutants in wastewater and surface water, such as persistent organic pollutants (POPs), pharmaceutical and personal care products (PPCPs) and endocrine disruptors, can directly or indirectly threaten human health owing to their high stability and toxicity [4,5]. Therefore, it is urgent to utilize an effective technology to eliminate these pollutants from aquatic environment. Up to now, many methods such as flocculation, adsorption, filtration, biodegradation and advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) have been used [6,7], among which AOPs have shown great potential for the elimination of organic contaminants due to the action of reactive oxygen species (ROS). Meanwhile, Fenton process is considered as one of the most popular technologies among AOPs because it owns outstanding advantages, efficient radical activity and easy operation [8].
The Fenton process was first proposed by H.J.H. Fenton in 1894 and has been developing rapidly in the field of water treatment these years (Eq. (1)). Although classical Fenton process possessed impressive degradation and mineralization ability towards organic pollutants, its applications are greatly restricted due to the following drawbacks: 1) excessive production of iron sludge; 2) high consumption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ; and 3) strict reaction conditions (optimum $\mathsf{p H}=3$ instead of neutral circumstance) [9]. To overcome these challenges, many researchers have paid much attention to the Fenton-like process, including photo-Fenton, sono-Fenton and electro-Fenton (EF) [10]. EF process is a promising wastewater treatment method and is drawing increasing interest, which can be reflected in Fig. 1. In this system, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ can be continuously supplied by Eqs. (2) and (3). Electricity is a renewable and clean energy source. Therefore, EF is also an environmentally friendly process for the remediation of water pollution [11,12].
$$
\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow{\cdot}0\mathrm{H}+\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{HO}^{-}
$$
$$
0_{2}+2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathsf{F e}^{3+}+\mathsf{e}^{-}\to\mathsf{F e}^{2+}
$$
For EF process, the efficiency of pollutant decay depends on the rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production and $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ regeneration at the cathode, and the working electrode was mainly composed of the electrode matrix and functional catalysts [13]. Thus, it is imperative to develop and use a class of advanced catalysts for the cathode to enhance the EF performance. According to the function of EF catalyst, its development can be divided into three categories: 1) the catalysts provide the source of ferrous iron, such as Fe nanoparticles (NPs) [14], $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ NPs [15]; 2) the materials are utilized for the electro-synthesis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ via $0_{2}$ at the cathode including various carbon materials (e.g., graphene [16], biochar [17] and carbon nanotubes [18]); 3) the catalysts play a dual role including 1) and 2). Previous studies have showed that carbon/heteroatom carbon based materials hold great promise in the electro-reduction $0_{2}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , compared to the methods of anthraquinone oxidation process, photo-catalyst or direct $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ synthesis from $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and $0_{2}$ [19– 22]. Iron (or other transition metal) embedded in the structure of solid-phase catalysts can broaden the pH range of Fenton reaction [23,24]. Therefore, seeking the carbon materials or metal/carbon materials with adequate catalytic activity and stability is a longterm challenge for future development of EF process.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are a novel class of highly porous crystalline materials, which are assembled by metal ions/- clusters and organic linkers through coordination bonds [25,26]. Recently, more than 20,000 MOFs with different metal center and/or organic linkers have been employed for energy storage [27–30], catalysis [31,32], adsorption [33], supercapacitor [34,35] and sensor [36] due to their high porosity, large specific surface area, tunable pore structure, diverse structure, controllable chemical and functional properties. Moreover, MOFs can be converted into metal oxides, porous carbon or metal/carbon composites through pyrolysis, which is an important way for further application of MOFs [37,38].
Remarkably, MOFs-based materials with versatile properties have been extensively used in EF process in recent years and this research field is rapidly growing and evolving. However, to the best of our knowledge, a comprehensive review summarizing the recent advances and progress made is still missing in the literature. Previously, Lu et al. reviewed the design and application of MOFs and derivatives as heterogeneous Fenton-like catalysts for organic wastewater treatment [39]. However, the review focused on the heterogeneous Fenton-like process rather than EF process. Therefore, in this work, we attempted to provide an overview of MOFs and their derivatives as catalysts for EF process in water purification. First, the fundamental mechanism of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ electro-synthesis via $2\mathsf{e}^{-}$ oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) was described in Section 2. Section 3 and 4 reviewed some synthetic methods of MOFs for EF process and some advantages of MOFs derivatives. Section 5 and 6 summarized the application of MOFs, MOFs composites and their derivatives (mainly porous carbon and metal/carbon composites) in water purification through EF process. In view of some defects such as poor conductivity and poor stability of MOFs, some robust strategies have also been mentioned to improve the catalytic efficiency of EF. In addition, the suitable pH and stability issue of MOFs derivatives for EF process were introduced in Section 6. Finally, the existing problems in this field and prospects were proposed in Section 8.
## 2. Mechanism of electro-synthesis of $\bf{H}_{2}O_{2}$ via $\mathbf{0_{2}}$
The ORR is multi-electron reaction, which involves several basic steps and intermediates (Fig. 2) [40]. Generally, there are three possible reaction mechanisms for ORR reaction: dissociation, association and peroxo mechanism [41–43]. The dissociation mechanism is that the O-O bond of oxygen breaks directly upon adsorption and the formed $0_{\mathsf{a d s}}$ is reduced to $\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ in turn. The association mechanism is to decompose the generated ${00}\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ into $\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ and $0_{\mathsf{a d s}}$ . The peroxo mechanism through $2\mathsf{e}^{-}$ transfer pathway leads to the formation of ${00}\mathrm{H}_{\mathbf{ads}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaHOOH_{ads}}$ successively, and the latter then decomposes into $\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ . Thus, the electro-synthesis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ involves the adsorption of dissolved $0_{2}$ by cathode, the formation of ${00}\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ intermediate and the acceptance of protons and electrons to produce $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ . It can be reduced to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by $2\mathsf{e}^{-}$ pathway when $0_{2}$ is adsorbed on the surface of cathode. However, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is an intermediate and a series of reactions (Eqs. 4–7) would take place after its formation on the cathode [44].
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}+2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathbf{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}\cdot+\mathbf{e}^{-}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{\mathrm{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}\cdot{\rightarrow}\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathbf{O}_{2}(\mathbf{g})+\mathbf{e}^{-}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}+\mathbf{O}_{2}(\mathbf{g})}}\end{array}
$$
## 3. Basic fabrication strategy for MOFs
MOFs is a new family of highly porous materials, which are composed of metal centers and organic ligands connected by coordination bonds. In the last two decades, some MOFs with good physicochemical properties were designed, such as MIL (Material of Institute Lavoisier) [52], ZIF (Zeolitic Imidazole Framework) [53,54], Prussian blue (PB) and its analogues (PBAs) [55,56]. Some representative MOFs structures and organic ligands are displayed in Fig. 3. As for the design strategy, the main purpose of MOFs synthesis is to establish the synthetic conditions for the generation of certain inorganic structural blocks without decomposition of organic linkers. The crystallization kinetics must be appropriate to allow for the phase required for nucleation and growth [57]. There are many methods to synthesize MOFs including solvo/hydrothermal [58], microwave heating [59], mechanochemical [60] ultrasound treatment and electrochemical synthesis [57]. The advantages and disadvantages of these methods are illustrated in Table 1. Obviously, synthesis parameters (e.g., metal salts, molar ratios of materials, solvent reaction time, pH and temperature) can influence the properties of these compounds including particle size, size distribution and morphology, resulting in the materials with significantly different properties [61]. Biemmi et al. utilized five different metal salts $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O},$ $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , $Z n S O_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O$ , $\mathrm{{ZnCl}}_{2}$ ) to synthesize MOF-5 [62]. By SEM images (Fig. 4a), they found that MOF-5 could be successfully prepared by employing $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ZnO. Employing $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ led to small crystals with well-shaped cubic morphology, which was ascribed to higher basicity in comparison with zinc nitrate and better solubility of the zinc source in comparison to the oxide. Meanwhile, the group explored the influence of synthesis temperature on the phase-purity and morphology of HKUST-1. They concluded that the morphology of HKUST-1 changed from platelets to inter-grown rhombohedra with increasing temperature and the crystal formed at higher temperatures do not present the well-defined octahedral morphology observed at $348\mathrm{~K~}$ and $393\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 4b). Chalati et al. explored the influence of different parameters (e.g., temperature, time, pH) on MIL-88A(Fe) synthesis [63]. From Fig. 4c, the particles became larger by dynamic light scattering (DLS) and TEM images upon temperature increase. Meanwhile, the yield also increased from $43\%$ to $60\%$ between 65 and $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The particles size of MIL-88A(Fe) increased from $110~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to $1030~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with the reaction time increased from $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The essential conditions for the synthesis of representative MOFs (especially Fe-MOFs) used in EF process are summarized in Table 2.
Undoubtedly, the development of MOFs is not limited to the synthesis of single MOFs. Its research hotspots also include the introduction of organic functional groups and bimetallic center as well as design of structures, MOFs composites and MOFs derivatives. These may lead to the formation of defects and catalytic sites with Lewis acids as well as the dispersion of metal centers, which are beneficial to the improvement of catalytic performance.
## 4. Advantages of using MOFs as the precursor for their derivatives
In general, it is difficult for simple metal salts to start as precursors to achieve accurately control over the pore size, surface area, shape or composition. The discovery of MOFs provides a step forward to the fine adjustment in catalyst synthesis [57]. Heteroatoms can be introduced into the structure of MOFs to improve the overall performance, because it is easy to modify and functionalize MOFs after synthesis. To some extent, the properties of micro/ nano-materials depend on the changes in structure, morphology, size, and crystal form during the preparation process, and the structure derived from MOFs provides the fine-tuning requirement to control these factors [30].
In the context of the synthesis of various nanostructures, MOFs can serve as the precursor in which the metal components provide original metal source to obtain the nanostructures of metal or metal oxides, and a self-sacrificing template in which the organic components could be utilized as carbon sources to obtain hierarchical porous carbon [64]. Generally, some MOFs including MOF5 and Cu-BTC, are prone to collapse during pyrolysis, whereas other MOFs like ZIF-67 and ZIF-8, can be better used as templates to form sponge-pore systems through evaporation of bound organic moisture during the carbonization process [65]. Some main advantages about MOFs as precursor for the synthesis of various nanostructure materials were summarized as follows: 1) Most MOFs derived materials may retain their morphologies at a relatively low pyrolysis temperature $(\sim500^{\circ}{\sf C})$ [66]. In particular, successful application of this strategy can cause the generation of hierarchical porous composite materials which have several micro/nano structures with diverse morphologies [30]. Thus, it can enhance the in-situ generation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , or increase the specific area to promote the mass transfer efficiency of pollutants in EF process. The complex composition and functionalized hybrid structure derived from MOFs significantly improve its electrochemical performance [67]. 2) MOFs-derived nanostructures with ideal topological structure (like core–shell and hollow structures) can be prepared by the controlled-calcination of MOFs and the ordered structure can lead to uniform distribution of the metal composition. In the EF system, the core–shell structure can avoid the oxidation of Fe(II) or facilitate the reduction of Fe(III), which is beneficial to the improvement in the stability of the catalysts [68]. The hollow structure of the materials can enhance the mass and electron transfer and diffusion, which is favorable to the obtainment of high electro-catalytic performance [69]. 3) The synthesis methods may address the potential incompatibility problem between electroactive materials and three dimensional (3D) substrate carriers through some special growth and decomposition mechanisms. 4) Theoretical calculations indicate the atoms on the edges and vertices of solid-phase catalysts have stronger catalytic activity than those on the surface [70]. With proper design control, MOFs derived materials can exhibit more exposure and provide more active sites in EF process. 5) Additionally, doping heteroatoms (N, P, O, F etc.) and the presence of hierarchical pores (micro-, meso-, and macropores) can exhibit better catalytic performance.
## 5. MOFs and their composites for the heterogeneous electroFenton process
According to the above mentioned multiple advantages of MOFs, transition metal based MOFs may show great potential as heterogeneous EF (hetero-EF) catalysts. However, almost no single pristine MOFs were used for EF process due to some drawbacks including poor conductivity and stability in water [71,72]. Therefore, it is necessary to make some modifications for MOFs in the application of EF.
The performance of MOFs-based hetero-EF process can be enhanced by combining MOFs with some functional materials. For example, Cheng et al. synthesized the Fe-Co-85 PBA ([Fe $(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]$ and $\left[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}\right]$ -based Prussian blue analogues) encapsulated by oxidized carbon nanotubes (OCNTs) and used it as part of the working electrode to degrade sulfamethazine (SMZ) in EF process [73]. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of OCNTs/PBA (Fig. 5a-b) indicated that Fe-Co-85 PBA micro-cube had six exposed {100} facets, and OCNTs were uniformly coated on the surface of PBA. They found that the ORR electron transfer number of OCNTs was 2.07 and the combination of Fe-Co-85 PBA did not obviously reduce the $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-}$ ORR performance of the OCNTs. Furthermore, the results showed that the electro-catalyst had a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ yield and SMZ can be completely degraded in $30\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 5c-d). Possible mechanism was proposed for explaining the improved EF performance: 1) ORR sites on OCNTs closely contacted with the Fenton active sites on Fe-Co-85 PBA; 2) bridging effect of OCNTs; 3) the interaction between oxygen-containing functional groups like COO-Fe complexes and $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}/F e^{\mathrm{III}}}$ on the surface of Fe-Co-85 PBA (Fig. 5e).
The performance of MOFs-based hetero-EF process can be also enhanced by the incorporation of photo energy. Irradiating wastewater with sunlight instead of UV lamps is a more costeffective photo-EF (PEF) method [74]. The first example of MOFs employed as an emerging catalyst for PEF process was reported by the group of Zhao in 2016 [75]. The authors utilized MOF $(2\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co})/$ carbon aerogel (iron and cobalt are the metal centers and the linker is trimesic acid, and carbon aerogel was used for the production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\cdot$ ) as the cathode to degrade rhodamine B (RhB) and dimethyl phthalate (DMP). SEM images showed that MOF (2Fe/Co) with rectangular shape successfully grew on the surface of carbon aerogel (Fig. 6a). They found that the introduction of visible light irradiation could increase the removal efficiency of RhB by $52\%$ within $45\mathrm{min}$ and DMP by $41\%$ within $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ compared to EF process. That’s because $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ behaving as electron acceptor can suppress the recombination of electron–hole pair, which is beneficial to the catalyst activity of MOF (2Fe/Co). Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) analysis (Fig. 6b) demonstrated that the excitation of MOF (2Fe/Co) by visible light was the main reason for the enhancement of Fenton-like reactivity (Fig. 6c). Additionally, the mechanisms for PEF process via MOF $(2\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co})/$ carbon aerogel cathode were proposed (Eqs. 8–12). Later, Wang et al. employed $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}$ bi-metal MOFs/carbon nanofibers composite membrane as the cathode to degrade perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) through solar PEF system and the removal efficiency of PFOA could reach $95\%$ in $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ at pH 3 (Fig. 6d-e) [76].
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{MOF(2Fe/Co)}+\mathrm{hv}\rightarrow\mathrm{e}_{\mathrm{cb}}^{-}+\mathrm{h}_{\mathrm{vb}}^{+}}\\ &{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{e}_{\mathrm{cb}}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{OH}}\\ &{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{hv}\rightarrow2\cdot\mathrm{OH}}\\ &{\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mp\mathrm{e}^{3+}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}+\cdot\mathrm{OH}}\\ &{\equiv\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mp\mathrm{e}^{2+}}\end{array}
$$
Generally, It is difficult to completely remove the residual soluble molecules of 3D MOFs from their micro-sized pore windows and they will limit the exposure of the coordination unsaturated site [77]. Engineering bulk MOFs into 2D MOFs could provide some typical advantages including high exposure of coordination unsaturated sites and large specific surface area, and thus facilitate the interaction between organic pollutants and active sites [78,79]. In addition to single layered MOFs, 2D MOFs also include those crystals with a high transverse to thickness aspect ratio that may have pores between the upper and bottom layers. A recent study conducted by Ye et al. reported the application of 2D Fe-MOFs with 2, $2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate (bpydc) as organic ligand in the PEF process for the degradation of bezafibrate [78]. The fieldemission SEM indicated the Fe-MOFs were successfully synthesized with a 2D irregular plate structure (Fig. 7a-c). Based on the experimental results, they found that 2D Fe-MOFs possessed better electro-oxidation ability compared to the 3D MOFs. The excellent catalytic activity can be explained by the following aspects (Fig. 7d): 1) the mass transfer of pollutants and the in-situ reduction of Fe(III) were enhanced in the form of 2D structure; 2) the photo-induced electron can decompose $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into OH and promote the regeneration of Fe(II); 3) the excitation and charge transfer in Fe-O clusters were improved.
## 6. MOFs derivatives for electro-Fenton process
In order to overcome their drawbacks (e.g., poor conductivity and stability), MOFs and their composites are usually converted into porous carbon materials or metal/carbon composites through a pyrolysis procedure [80,81]. In general, nano-porous metal/carbon composites can be obtained by carbonizing MOFs under the protection of inert atmosphere (e.g., Ar, ${\sf N}_{2}$ ). It can produce porous carbon materials and improve the specific surface area of the materials by further etching the residual metal species off through strong acid $\mathrm{\cdotHNO}_{3}$ , HF). Sometimes, the latter procedure is unnecessary because metal will be separated from the composite in the gaseous form when the carbonation temperature is higher than its boiling point. Unfortunately, the structure of MOFs may appear cavities or even collapse during pyrolysis due to its low carbon content and anisotropic shrinkage [82]. In order to address these drawbacks, it is necessary to co-pyrolyze MOFs with other carbon sources (such as furfuryl alcohol [83] and glucose [84]) or to use more stable MOFs.
For instance, Liu and co-workers first reported the employment of MOFs as precursor for nano-porous carbon synthesis in 2008 [85]. They used MOF-5 $\left(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{OOCC}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{COO})_{3}\right)$ as precursor and furfuryl alcohol as additional carbon sources for preparing nanoporous carbon through high-temperature carbonization $(1000~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . Therefore, the Zn metal (boiling point $908^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ ) evaporated with the flow of Ar and the remaining carbon species eventually turned into nano-porous carbon sample, and the BET surface area of nitrogen-doped porous carbon (NPC) sample was $2872~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was similar to a double-sided separated graphitic sheet $(2965\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\nonumber$ ). Later, the group of Hu used Al-based MOFs as a self-template for preparing nano-porous carbon for the first time [86]. In the experiment, the Al-MOFs were carbonized directly in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere without the addition of other carbon sources and the obtained nano-porous carbon was washed with HF to remove the residual Al component. Since then, extensive efforts have focused on the development of MOFs derivatives. Herein, we emphasize on the application progress of MOFs derivatives as new catalysts in EF process.
## 6.1. Carbon materials
Recently, carbon materials are identified as emerging alternatives to noble metals for the electro-catalytic synthesis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ because they are inexpensive, earth-abundant and possess unique features [87–89]. Porous carbon derived from MOFs template (e.g., MOF-5, MIL-53(Al)) exhibit some excellent properties including tunable pore structure, large surface area and good electrochemical performance [85,86]. Quan and co-workers first used MOF-5 as template to obtain hierarchically porous carbon (HPC) under $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ atmosphere for high-yield electro-synthesis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ via $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-}$ ORR pathway [90]. Since then, porous carbon materials prepared by pristine MOFs have been widely studied as new electrocatalysts for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation and water depollution through EF treatment, and they were summarized in Table 3.
The porous carbon derived from MOFs can be directly used to enhance the electro-synthesis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The first example of MOFs used as template to obtain porous carbon for the electrochemical $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production was studied by the group of Quan in 2015 [90]. The authors used MOF-5 to obtain HPC with abundant micro-, meso-, or even macropores (Fig. 8a-b). The abundant pore structure of HPC was conducive to the mass transfer of ORR species $(0_{2}/\mathrm{OH}^{-})$ and the exposure of active sites. They concluded that the main product in the process of HPC electro-catalysis for ORR was $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . High-yield $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production was obtained with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ accumulation of $222.6\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{p H}1$ . Meanwhile, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ selectivity of HPC electro-catalysis reached $80.9\%-\ 95.0\%$ under the optimal operating condition, which was similar to those of precious metal based electro-catalysts (Fig. 8c-d). Through the characterization of $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), Raman spectroscopy and calculation of Density functional theory (DFT), it can be concluded that the outstanding performance of HPC was mainly due to the following factors: 1) the high content of defects (marginal and vacancy type) and $\mathsf{s p}^{3}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{C};2$ ) 3D hierarchical porous structure and high surface area. Following this work, this group utilized the HPC/carbon paper as the cathode for the degradation of PFOA by homogeneous EF process, and obtained excellent mineralization efficiency $90.7\%$ in $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) (Fig. 8e) [91].
More interestingly, porous carbon materials derived from MOF-5 and ZIF-8 have been applied for EF-based membrane fouling control technologies and other combined processes. From the perspective of practical application, the in-situ control of membrane fouling through EF process is a desirable and promising method, which can destroy the pollutants on the membrane surface and even in the membrane pores. The group of Quan first utilized porous carbon derived from ZIF-8, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and polyvinyl butyral (PVB) to prepare PC-CNT hollow fiber membrane for membrane fouling control [101]. As can be seen in Fig. 10a and b, PC-CNT hollow fiber membrane was successfully prepared without any structural breakage. The results showed that the removal rate of phenol, glucose, and protein through PC-CNT hollow fiber membrane based EF was approximately 9.7, 6.2 and 2.7 times higher than that of membrane filtration alone (Fig. 10ce). Moreover, there were two advantages of this coupled system. Firstly, OH produced by EF can attack the contaminants deposited on the surface of membrane; Secondly, the effect of electrical repulsion can form an electric barrier near the membrane, and thus reduce the deposition of oppositely charged contaminants on the surface of membrane (Fig. 10f-h). Similarly, this research group used the PC-CNT hollow fiber membrane for scenario-specific application (microalgae harvesting, a promising source of biofuel), and the PC-CNT hollow fiber membrane in the presence of electricity played a bi-functional role of harvesting microalgae and in-situ mitigating membrane fouling resulted from microalgae cells and extracellular organic matter (EOM) (Fig. 10i) [102]. In addition, this research group utilized the system in the membrane bioreactors, and PC (derived by ZIF-8), CNTs and $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ were sealed into hollow fiber membrane (Fe-PC-CHFM) acting as the cathode (Fig. 11a) [103]. As can be observed from the SEM images of Fe-PC-CHFM (Fig. 11b-c), the membrane had obvious network structure at the end of operation, which was similar to the initial level state and the results suggested that the Fe-PC-CHFM bioreactor with electrolysis could achieve $93\%$ COD and $88\%\ \mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}.$ -N removal after 100-day operation. Moreover, homogeneous-EF based microbial fuel cell (ZIF-8 derived PC cathode) for water depollution was also reported. The group of Cretin used PC (derived by ZIF-8)/CF as EF based fuel cell cathode for the degradation of AO7, and this system was driven by the direct generation of electrons through the oxidation of glucose without any external power supply (Fig. 11d) [104]. As shown in Fig. 11e, $90\%$ AO7 degradation efficiency was obtained in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ by the coupled process.
## 6.2. Metal/carbon composites
As mentioned earlier, MOFs derived porous carbon is a promising material for EF process toward pollutants degradation. Since adding iron salt would greatly hamper the practical application of EF because of the precipitation of iron ions under neutral conditions, extensive studies have focused on transition metal porous carbon materials (TMPCs), especially Fe based porous carbon materials. MOFs with ordered structures and compositions are the ideal precursors to prepare TMPCs. Previous works on MOFs-derived metal/carbon composites employed in EF process for the organic pollutants removal were summarized in Table 4.
Among the various types of reported MOFs, MIL-type Fe-based MOFs have been extensively utilized as precursor to prepare Febased porous carbon materials for water depollution through EF process. To date, a large number of derivatives have been developed from different MIL-type Fe-based MOFs template via utilizing multiple types of organic ligands. Recently, Liu and co-workers used microwave-assisted method to prepare different kinds of Fe-MOFs (MIL-101(Fe), MIL-101(Fe)– ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , MIL-100(Fe), MIL-88 (Fe), MIL-88(Fe)– $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ), and then Fe-MOFs were mixed with porous carbon matrix and carbonized at high temperature to obtain CMOF@PCM, which was used for EF degradation of herbicides [59]. It was found that the order of removal efficiency of napropamide by five MOFs precursors followed the order M $\mathrm{L}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})>\mathrm{MI}$ L-88(Fe $\mathrm{)-NH_{2}>M I L-88(F e)>M I L-101(F e)>M I L-101(F e)-N H_{2}}$ Furthermore, the morphology and reactivity of $\mathsf{C M O F@P C M}$ electrocatalyst depended on the specific MOFs precursor. For example, CMIL- $88\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ prepared by MIL-88(Fe)– $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ presented a porous core–shell structure consisting of a cementite intermediate layer sandwiched between magnetite core and graphite shell (Fig. 12ac). Later, the group of Quan synthesized $\mathrm{FeO_{x}/N H P C750}$ , a bifunctional catalyst, through the pyrolysis of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) for hetero-EF reaction (Fig. 12d) [58,105]. SEM and TEM images indicated that FeOx/NHPC750 electro-catalysts exhibited the hexagonal rod-like morphology and possessed a porous structure (Fig. 12e-g). The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ selectivity of FeOx/NHPC750 was found to be $95\%{-}98\%$ , which was much higher than that of FeOx/HPC750 (Fig. 12h). Notably, as illustrated in the Fig. 12i, the phenol was almost completely removed by $\mathrm{FeOx}/\mathrm{HPC}750$ hetero-EF process within $120\ \mathrm{min}$ . Lu and co-workers carbonized the mixture of MIL-100(Fe) and polyaniline to obtain $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}/\Nu_{-}C$ carbon paper cathode for EF degradation of bisphenol A (BPA) (Fig. 12j-l) [106]. In addition, many metal/hierarchical porous carbon materials derived from other MIL type Fe-based MOFs like MIL-53(Fe) [107] and MIL-101(Fe) [108] have been coated on the electrode matrix for EF degradation of contaminants in wastewater.
Compared with mono-metal Fe-based carbon materials, carbon-based materials containing two or more kinds of metals is regard to be promising due to the faster electron-transfer among multivalent metals. Meanwhile, the introduction of non-iron Fenton-type catalyst (mainly non-iron metals) will also promote the production of OH (Fig. 13a) [10]. To date, researchers have combined a number of MOFs-derived bimetallic porous carbon materials with the electrode matrix for degradation of organic pollutants by EF process including Fe/Cu [109], Fe/Co [110], Fe/Mn [111] and Fe/Ce [112] (Fig. 13a-d). The incorporation of these non-iron metals significantly improved the removal efficiency of pollutants and the mechanisms can be summarized by the following aspects: 1) the electron transfer between $\mathrm{\equiv}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II/III}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{I/II}/C o^{I I/I I I}/\Delta M n^{I I/I I I}/C e^{I I I/I V}}$ (Eqs. 13–16) promotes the regeneration of Fenton active sites, which is conducive to the formation of OH; 2) these bimetallic nanoparticles embedded in porous carbon can prevent agglomeration, increase active sites and reduce metal ion leaching; 3) porous carbon framework with abundant defects can improve the electrical conductivity, benefiting to the formation and activation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Nevertheless, some issues about MOFs derived metal/porous carbon materials as the cathode components for EF still need to be noted: 1) given that the material is a bifunctional heterogeneous catalyst, a balance between the reaction sites of the carbon material and the reaction sites of the transition metal is required; 2) other non-iron bimetallic such as ${\sf C u}/{\sf C o},{\sf M n}/$ Co might be promising alternative approaches. Recently, Li and coworkers used $\mathrm{Fe/Co/Zn@C-NCFs@CF}$ cathode for EF degradation of enrofloxacin $'{\mathrm{Fe}}/{\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{Zn}}@C$ was prepared by $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ co-doped ZIF through pyrolysis) [113].
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{III}}+\equiv\mathbf{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\to\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}+\equiv\mathbf{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}}\\ &{\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}+\equiv\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}\to\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}+\equiv\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}}\\ &{\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}+\equiv\mathsf{M n}^{\mathrm{II}}\to\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}+\equiv\mathsf{M n}^{\mathrm{II}}}\\ &{\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}+\equiv\mathbf{Ce}^{\mathrm{IV}}\to\equiv\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{III}}+\equiv\mathbf{Ce}^{\mathrm{III}}}\end{array}
$$
Generally, carbonization under harsh conditions (mainly high temperature) is the most common method to obtain MOFs derivatives (mainly porous carbon based materials). However, the total decomposition of pristine MOFs usually leads to a sharp decrease of surface area and the destruction of MOFs’ pores/channel structures. The decomposition of metal nodes in MOFs may result in the aggregation of metal sites in the 3D structure, which blocked the entrance of the reactants into the active center [114]. As a consequence, the trade-off between abundant active sites and satisfactory conductivity is an issue that should be solved. Currently, ‘‘partially controlled pyrolysis” by carefully controlling heat treatment temperature (usually below $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ is considered as an attractive method to create specific MOFs derivatives with reasonable design [115]. Wang and co-workers prepared NiFe MOF nanosheets (MOF-NFs-temperature) for efficient electrochemical synthesis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ through partially controlled pyrolysis strategy (Fig. 14a) [116]. According to TEM images (Fig. 14b), they concluded that small NPs appeared at $250^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , and the morphology of the pristine NiFe MOF NSs would be destructed at $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and a partial decomposition occurred on the MOF NSs at $300^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ . At the same time, they found that the MOF NSs-300 showed the highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ selectivity through $2\mathrm{e}^{-}$ ORR pathway (Fig. 14c), which was much higher than that of NiFe@C/graphite electrode (NiFe MOF was carbonized at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [117]. Finally, compared to the completely pyrolyzed products, the mechanism was proposed: the improvement of MOF NSs-300 electro-catalytic activity was mainly due to the retention of MOFs structure and the generation of NiO NPs (Fig. 14d), which contributed to the formation of coordination unsaturated Ni atoms and regulated the catalytic properties during electro-synthesis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . More recently, Dong and co-workers utilized quasi-MIL-101(Fe) derivative-based electrode for effective hetero-EF degradation of $\mathfrak{p}$ -nitrophenol through a pyrolysis step at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 15a) [118]. As shown in Fig. 15b-d, CFP@PANI@ MIL-101 (400) EF system achieved the best removal efficiency ( $100\%$ in $120\ \mathrm{min};$ of $\mathfrak{p}$ -nitrophenol among all the temperatures (from $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $800^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ ) and the modified cathode exhibited excellent $2\mathsf{e}^{-}$ ORR selectivity compared to the others, which was conducive to the Fenton reaction. The associated mechanisms were displayed in Fig. 15e: 1) Fe-O sites in MIL-101(400) can be used as active sites for $0_{2}$ adsorption and activation to improve the electrochemical production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ; 2) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is converted to OH under the catalysis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and fast electron transfer at the cathode; 3) part of p-nitrophenol can be decomposed by electro-catalytic process on the boron-doped diamond (BDD) anode. Moreover, the group of Chen used MIL-101(Fe)-310 T@nickel foam $(310\mathrm{T}$ means that MIL-101(Fe) was pyrolyzed at $310^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for the degradation of diclofenac (DCF) by EF process [119]. The results showed that the removal of DCF was $92\%$ within $90~\mathrm{{min}}$ under neutral condition. Furthermore, they concluded that the partly thermal treatment of MIL-101(Fe) can maintain original crystal structure and expose more Fe active sites and oxygen vacancies, which was conducive to the production of ROS including OH and ${}^{1}0_{2}$ (Fig. 15f).
Since MOFs-derived metal/porous carbon materials have been used as bi-functional hetero-EF catalysts, some researchers also explored their usage as suspended catalysts for hetero-EF process to remove organic pollutants from aqueous environment. As suspended catalysts do not participate in the in-situ production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , these catalysts should have some advantages including rapid decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , facile recycling, rapid electron transfer and stable structure to meet the requirements of accelerating EF reaction. Undoubtedly, magnetic nanoparticle as suspended catalyst is a research hotspot because it can be easily separated and recycled by applying a magnetic field. Recently, Ye and co-workers synthesized magnetic N-doped nano- $.Z{\mathsf{V I@C}}$ (prepared by MIL-type Fe-MOFs) as suspended catalysts for the removal of gemfibrozil by EF process (Fig. 17a) [68]. The carbonization process can transform Fe(III) into $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{0}$ successively, and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC can act as a reducing agent to promote the production of $\mathsf{F e}^{0}$ [126]. From
TEM images (Fig. 17b), it was found that nano- $.Z{\mathsf{V I@C-N}}$ catalyst preserved a typical bi-pyramidal rod-like morphology. The existence of core–shell structure can minimize the oxidation of magnetite and ZVI and avoid the deposition of iron ions on the surface of catalysts. Compared with other treatments, the EF process catalyzed by nano-ZVI@C-N exhibited excellent catalytic performance with gemfibrozil removal ratio higher than $95\%$ at $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Fig. 17c). Fig. 17d demonstrated the excellent cycling stability of the nano-ZVI@N-C catalyst. Magnetization curve (Fig. 17e) showed typical hysteresis loops of ferromagnetic materials at room temperature. Hence, the synthesized materials can be recovered effectively by permanent magnet or applied magnetic field. However, magnetism also makes iron atoms from MOFs derivatives agglomerate easily, which could hinder the catalytic activity. To solve the dispersion of magnetic oxide nanoparticles, $\mathsf{C u@C}$ NPs (derived by HKUST-1) embedded in 3D reduced graphene oxide (rGO) network as hetero-EF catalyst for the degradation of RhB was prepared by the group of Yang in 2019 (Fig. 18a) [127]. As clearly observed in Fig. 18b-c, the 3D high-porosity structure and the $\mathsf{C u@C}$ nanoparticles were uniformly distributed in the 3D rGO network. Fig. 18d showed that RhB was completely removed in $150\ \mathrm{min}$ through $3\mathsf{D G}/\mathsf{C u@C}$ hetero-EF process. Moreover, 3D rGO network could prevent the aggregation of Cu NPs and facilitate the contact of organic pollutants with radicals during hetero-EF process (Fig. 18e). This study provided a promising approach to synthesize hetero-EF catalysts. Later, $\mathrm{{Cu/N{-}C}}$ materials derived from $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOFs have also been used as suspend catalysts for degradation of BPA by hetero-EF process (Fig. 18f) [128].
In recent years, some researchers utilized natural sulfide minerals (e.g., pyrite, chalcopyrite) as EF catalysts in water purification and excellent mineralization has been achieved [129–131]. In contrast to the traditional EF process, pyrite-EF system was reported to exhibit better catalytic performance due to the following advantages: 1) the pH of solution can be adjusted to the appropriate value by itself, and an efficient circulation between $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ is possible (Eqs. 17–19) [132]; 2) pyrite is abundant on earth and easy to recycle. The pyrite based Fenton process has exhibited excellent catalytic ability, but it has the disadvantage of excessive iron leaching. It can affect the reusability of catalysts and bring the problem of sludge management. In this context, synthesized $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}$ and other iron sulfides have shown a longer cycling performance and better catalytic efficiency. In particular, synthesizing MOFs engineered $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}$ (or $\mathsf{C u F e S}_{2})/\mathsf{C}$ nanocomposite with core–shell structure, prepared by simultaneous sulfidation and carbonization of Fe-MOFs (or $\mathtt{C u}/$ Fe MOFs) precursor, may improve the stability of the material and the efficiency of catalytic degradation. Recently, Ye and co-workers carbonized the mixture of Fe-MOFs and sulfur to obtain $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}/C$ nanocomposite for the degradation of fluoxetine through hetero-EF process at mild pH (Fig. 19a) [133]. As illustrated in Fig. 19b, pyrite $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}$ was the only sulfide remaining on the catalyst surface. The catalyst had a core–shell structure with $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}$ and carbon shells in the inner and outer layer respectively, which greatly improved the stability and catalytic performance of $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}$ . They found that the iron leaching of the synthetic catalyst $\cdot1.5\mathrm{\mg\L}^{-1}$ ) was much lower than that of natural pyrite based EF process $\langle8{\mathrm{~mg~L}}^{-1}$ ) and the catalytic hetero-EF process of $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ (removal ratio of $91\%$ in $60\mathrm{{min}}$ ) was significantly superior to other treatments (Fig. 19c). The following mechanisms were proposed to elucidate the obtained results (Fig. 19d): 1) the new catalyst had a large specific surface area; 2) the high content of Fe(II) on the surface of $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}$ induced the activation of molecular $0_{2}$ , thereby promoting the production of superoxide radical; 3) the presence of N doping enhanced the mass transport.
