6.3. Effects of electrode matrix and binding mode on catalytic performance of MOFs-derivatives based EF process
In addition to the above mentioned MOFs as precursors for EF process, some researchers attempted to select different electrode carriers and different combination modes between MOFs and electrode matrix to further promote the degradation efficiency of pollutants. On the one hand, choosing suitable cathode substrate, including carbon felt (CF), graphite felt (GF), activated carbon fiber (ACF), graphite sheet and reticulated vitreous carbon foam, to replace carbon paper as electrode matrix to load porous carbon material derived from MOFs [117,134–137]. The synergistic effects of porous carbon and electrode matrix can further improve the yield of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and thus enhance the removal rate of organic pollutants. Recently, Yu and co-workers coated NPC, which was prepared by the carbonization of ZIF-8 s, on the GF for homogeneous EF degradation of phenol (Fig. 20a) [138]. As expected, under the same conditions, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ yield of the NPC modified cathode was 10 times higher than that of the original GF (Fig. 20b). Phenol could be completely removed and TOC removal rate reached $82.6\%$ in $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ . They concluded that the presence of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon and graphitic-N in NPC enhanced the electron transfer between $0_{2}$ molecules and carbon surface, which was helpful to improve the electro-catalytic reactivity. Subsequently, this research group coated HPC (derived from MOF-5) and NPC (derived from ZIF-8) respectively on ACF for homogeneous EF to remove phenol and tetracycline (TC) from wastewater, and it was found that the modified ACF cathode could completely remove these pollutants in $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ [139,140].
On the other hand, changing the bonding mode between porous carbon and electrode matrix instead of just coating porous carbon on the electrode supports. The traditional electrode preparation method is mainly coating the electrode matrix with slurry manually (e.g., droplet coating and spraying), and the slurry usually contains electro-catalysts, conductive material (e.g., carbon black, acetylene black) and binder (e.g., Nafion, polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE)). Some thorny issues will limit the application of traditional electrode preparation method in the electrochemical field [141]: 1) the binder may interfere with the transfer kinetics of ions and electrons at the interface of electrode/electrolyte or in the inner of electrode; 2) most of the electrically active surface of electrode may be prevented from contacting the electrolyte/pollutants due to the existence of binder; 3) the active material is easy to detach from the current collector after multiple tests. By contrast, in-situ growth method may overcome these shortcomings and improve the electrochemical performance of the electrode including the aspects of electrical conductivity, stability and exposure of active sites, and, consequently, many binder-free composite cathodes have been employed in EF process [112,142,143]. The group of Marc fabricated NPC/CF cathode for refractory pollutant removal through a three-step strategy (atomic layer deposition of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , solvent-thermal conversion to ZIF-8, NPC/CF obtained by high temperature carbonization) (Fig. 20c) [144]. SEM images showed that the composite material transforms into a NPC thin layer on the CF surface and the NPC preserved the original polyhedral shape of ZIF-8 (Fig. 20d-f). The apparent rate constant of acid orange (AO7) degradation by NPC/CF was $0.8\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , and the TOC removal of AO7 remained a high value after ten cycling tests, indicating excellent stability of the cathode. Wei et al. developed a binder-free and self-supporting EF electrode [145] (Fig. 20g). From the SEM and TEM images (Fig. 20h-i), it can be observed that ZIF-8 derived carbon nanotube-like materials grew uniformly on the surface of the 3D nickel foam, which was conducive to the formation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .
6.4. Effects of pH on the catalytic performance of MOFs derivativesbased EF process
Generally, the optimal pH range of classical homogeneous Fenton process is 2.8–3.5 [5]. However, most of the real wastewater is around neutral pH range [146–148]. Therefore, it is necessary to explore the influence of initial pH value on the catalytic performance of MOFs derivatives for EF process. Several studies have suggested that MOFs derivatives-based EF catalysis can work in a wide pH range. For instance, Zhang and co-workers reported that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ produced in situ by NPC (ZnPDA derivatives) can be decomposed through extra ferrous added in a wide pH range, from 1 to 7 [93]. However, notably, the removal efficiency of OXC decreased with the increase of the initial pH value. A similar result was also found in a recent study, in which FPC (MOF-53(Al) derivatives) EF system can also exhibit excellent catalytic performance at mild pH [97]. Besides, pH appears to have a greater effect on
MOFs-derived metal/carbon EF system. It is found that an increase of initial pH value from 3 to 5 had significant influence on the catalytic ability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}/{\sf N}-C$ (MIL-100(Fe)) in EF treatment [106]. The combination of Fe and other transition metal (Mn and Cu) could broaden the pH range in EF process. For example, Zhou and coworkers reported that the removal ratio of triclosan (TCS) in $\mathsf{M n}/$ ${\mathrm{Fe@PC}}$ modified cathode EF system decreased by only $10\%$ as pH increased from 3 to 7, which was attributed to the instability of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under neutral or alkaline conditions [111]. Cao and coworkers reported that phenol could be completely removed within $90~\mathrm{{min}}$ in $\mathrm{FeO_{x}/C u N H P C}$ modified cathode EF system over a broad pH range (from 4 to 10) [105]. Additionally, desirable structures (such as core–shell) or combinations with graphene oxide can also broaden the range of pH applications of the catalyst in EF [68,127]. Based on the results from published literature, it is reasonable to find that MOFs derivatives-based EF process exhibits good reactivity in the pH range 3–8.
## 6.5. Stability and reusability of MOFs derivatives-based EF process
The stability of MOFs derivative catalysts is an important indicator worthy of investigation, whether they are used as active components of the cathode or suspended catalysts for EF process. On the one hand, whether the structure of catalyst is destroyed after reaction is an important index to judge its stability. Thus, the characterization (SEM, XRD, XPS, etc.) of catalysts before and after reaction provides an important basis for the stability of MOFs derived catalysts. For example, through XRD patterns and SEM images, Dong and co-workers found that $\mathrm{CFP@PANI@MIL}\boldsymbol{1}01(400)$ cathode barely changed after EF process, which presented great advantages for the long-term application of wastewater treatment [118]. Du and co-workers reported that the chemical composition of catalysts had no noticeable change after five consecutive tests through XPS spectra and the used $\mathrm{Fe/Fe_{3}C@P C}$ returned to the initial catalytic performance, which was mainly due to the thermal treatment of catalyst enabling the reduction of the iron oxide on its surface [108]. On the other hand, the release of excessive metal ions will not only affect the recycling performance of the catalyst, but also pollute the water as a secondary contaminant. Thus, it is necessary to measure the amount of metal ions dissolved from the catalyst after the EF reaction. It has been reported that the amount of metal ion leaching usually increased with the increase of reaction time and the decrease of pH value [149,150]. Nevertheless, according to the previous studies, the leaching concentration of metal ion after EF process for MOFs-derived hetero-EF catalysts was relatively low, usually in the range of $0.02{-}2\ \mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The small leaching of metal ion is probably attributed to the special structure of catalysts such as core–shell structure, hollow porous structure and the protection of carbon matrix [59,68,105]. For instance, Liu and co-worker found that the leaching of Fe from CMIL$100@\mathrm{PCM}$ was much lower than that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{PCM}$ after $120\ \mathrm{min}$ hetero-EF reaction, which was presumably ascribed to the core–shell structure [59]. In another study, after $90\ \mathrm{min}$ EF reaction with hierarchical $\mathsf{M n/F e@P C}$ catalyst, the concentrations of Fe and Mn leaching were only 0.705 and $0.308~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , respectively, implying that iron and copper species were relatively stable during the test. In homogeneous EF reaction, TCS removal efficiency $(4.35\%)$ was negligible indicating that catalytic performance of $\mathsf{M n/F e@P C}$ was dominated by hetero-EF catalysis [111]. From the perspective of potential practical applications, the reusability of MOFs-based EF catalysts is another important issue that needs to be considered. As summarized in Table 5, generally, the prepared MOFs derivatives or their modified cathodes were very stable and can be recycled multiple times for the treatment of organic pollutants.
## 7. Environmental risk of MOF materials
In EF process, MOFs might be released into the actual water environment, whether they are used as electrode materials or as suspended catalysts. Therefore, the environmental risks of MOFs should be investigated prior to the large-scale application. It is important to confirm the toxicity of MOFs to assess their environmental safety. Some studies have explored the toxicity of MOFs towards animals, plants and algae. Samon-Yarza et al. studied the in vivo behavior of MIL-100(Fe) after intravenous administration to rats [151]. By high performance liquid chromatography and a spectrophotometric method, they found that MIL-100(Fe) accumulated in liver and spleen to induce low hepatic toxicity after fast clearance from blood stream. Similarly, Baati et al. found that three different Fe-MOFs accumulated in lungs, spleen and liver, but only aroused oxidative stress rather than toxicity [152]. Deng et al. concluded that nano-scale ZIF-67 could impair learning and memory ability without noticeable toxicity in a rat [153]. Guan et al. studied the photosynthetic and toxicity of MOF-199 to pea seedlings [154]. They concluded that MOF-199 decreased the total photosynthesis capability and inhibited the seedling growth at high concentration. At the same time, Fan et al. studied the growing inhibition of Microcystic aeruginosa by MOFs [155]. They concluded that MOFs can be used to inhibit the growth of Microcystic aeruginosa and the toxicity followed the sequence of Cu-MOF- $\cdot74>\mathsf{A g}/$ $\mathsf{A g C l@Z I F-8}>\mathsf{Z I F-8}>\mathsf{Z n-M O F-74}$ These literatures indicated that MOFs materials might induce some negative influence to creatures or plants. The toxicity of MOFs (especially Fe-MOFs, Cu-MOFs and Co-MOFs) should be studied more systematically to ensure human health and environmental safety.
## 8. Conclusions and outlook
In conclusion, MOFs, as a novel class of porous crystalline materials, have revolutionized the field of Fenton oxidation process in recent years. Owing to the key advantages of large specific surface area, highly ordered structures and chemical stability of MOFs, MOFs-based materials have been regarded as promising alternative catalysts for EF process. In addition, there are many kinds of MOFs and various preparation methods. Many efforts have been concentrated on the adjustment of chemical structure and composition through changing metal centers or organic linkers. However, although the original MOFs can be used for a variety of applications, they are usually limited by their poor intrinsic conductivity. There are limited literatures about pristine MOFs for EF/PEF process. In order to overcome these drawbacks, proper pyrolysis of the pristine MOFs is extremely necessary. At the same time, the porous carbon, metal/carbon composites derived from MOFs have expanded the application of MOFs-based materials in the field of EF process. In this work, the recent developments on MOFs and MOFs-derived materials for EF process have been reviewed.
To date, tremendous advance has been achieved on the research of MOFs derived nanostructured materials for EF process and this area is keeping going forward rapidly. Typical MOFs like MILs and ZIFs have been extensively employed as precursors to synthesize derivatives with excellent catalytic performance for selective $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production and effective degradation of organic pollutants. Additionally, they have a wide pH application range and good chemical stability. However, MOFs and their derivatives for the EF process are still in the infancy. Further researches are necessary to make full of the excellent properties.
1. For the pristine MOFs, whether mono-metal or bimetallic, 2D or 3D structure, it is necessary to introduce photo-energies (UV light or visible light) to improve the degradation performance of EF. Another strategy is to combine MOFs with other suitable materials including OCNTs, graphene, carbon black, which are conducive to the $2\mathrm{e}^{-}$ ORR and the electron transport.
2. In the EF reaction, selective production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the regeneration of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ are rate-limiting steps. As the active component of cathode, MOFs tend to have low yield of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and insufficient active sites, leading to low catalytic degradation efficiency of pollutants. Carbonization of MOFs is favorable to the increase of exposed active sites, specific surface area and electrical conductivity. Heteroatom doping can promote the in-situ generation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at cathode. Meanwhile, bimetal MOFs derivatives may also provide more active sites for the EF treatment.
3. The complete carbonization of MOFs crystals may lead to the loss of original excellent properties of MOFs and the agglomeration of metal sites. Meanwhile, it is difficult to control the fine structures due to the unclear pyrolytic conversion mechanisms. Hence, a controlled partial pyrolysis strategy might offer a new route for the synthesis of MOFs-derived EF catalysts, which can inherit the advantages of the original MOFs and MOFs derivatives with excellent catalytic performance and good stability [156]. In addition, MOFs can be wrapped or supported by $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , polymer (such as polydopamine) and rGO to avoid inward shrinkage of organics and particle aggregation during carbonization process [82,157]. This would produce core–shell structure or hollow porous structure to protect the original framework of the MOFs.
4. MOFs-derived SACs have the advantages of homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts and have been widely used in the electro-catalytic field. Currently, Cao and co-workers found that the degradation kinetics of Fe atom/PC towards SMX was 59 times higher than that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{PC}$ through EF process [124]. Hence, MOFs-derived SACs for EF process is a new and promising research direction.
5. Conventional electrodes are fabricated by coating slurry containing active material, conductive material and binder on the electrode substrate followed by drying or pressing. Apart from adding dead mass, the presence of binder may block a large amount of pores of the active materials. Moreover, the active materials are easy to detach from the electrode substrate. Insitu growth (binder-free method) may be an effective measure to overcome these defects.
6. The metal/carbon materials derived from MOFs are promising as bi-functional EF catalysts for the elimination of organic pollutants. However, reducing the cost of MOFs synthesis and increasing the output of MOFs derivatives are still great challenges. Meanwhile, few studies investigated the energy consumption of MOFs-based EF system.
7. In MOFs-based EF process, most studies focus on the degradation of dye pollutants and antibiotics, whereas a wider range of POPs such as PCPs and perfluorinated organic compounds should be selected to test the degradation ability of MOFsbased EF. It is also suggested to replace simulated wastewater with real sewage and to determine the degradation efficiency by TOC removal ratio [158].
8. Current relevant researches have been conducted under laboratory scenarios. Tests utilizing pilot-scale or large-scale equipment have not been carried out. Thus, more work should be done to acquire information about scalability, operational cost, system lifetime, and any issue arising from the scale-up. Appropriate pre and post treatment methods need to be explored.
9. In view of the cost aspects and environmental friendliness, MIL type Fe-MOFs and their derivatives have the potential to achieve industrial applications in the future. Combing MOFsbased EF process with other technologies (such as membrane bioreactor and microbial fuel cell) may expand its application scope and speed up its practical application.
